[38;2;124;124;124mexists a point p such that an open neighborhood of π(p) in M/G is smooth.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have a Lie group G acting smoothly and effectively on a smooth manifold M. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe quotient map π: M → M/G. We need to determine if there exists a point p ∈ M [0m[38;2;124;124;124msuch that an open neighborhood of π(p) in M/G is smooth (i.e., admits a smooth [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmanifold structure).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- G acts smoothly and effectively on M.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The action need not be free or proper.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the quotient M/G to be a smooth manifold near π(p), we typically need the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124maction to be free and proper near p (or at least the orbit through p to be a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclosed submanifold and the slice theorem to apply).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe slice theorem (Palais): If G is compact (or action is proper) and the action[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis smooth, then near any point with free action (trivial isotropy), there's a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mslice giving a local smooth structure on the quotient.[0m

